     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Acetaminophen at 15 g/day is well into the range that causes acute liver injury and potentially liver failure. It's also above the single-dose toxicity threshold of about 150 mg/kg, which is roughly 10.5 g for a 70 kg patient. I don't know of any indication where this dosing is therapeutic, and you haven't given a rationale. Normal liver function doesn't make it safe.\n\nI can still help in these ways:\n- **Enter a standard dose right away.** For example, 1000 mg PO every 8 hours (3 g/day) or 1000 mg PO every 6 hours (4 g/day).\n- **Look into what you're trying to treat.** If the goal is stronger analgesia or antipyresis, I can enter a multimodal regimen. If this is a possible overdose, the right step is an acetaminophen level and N-acetylcysteine, and I'd suggest calling poison control.\n- **Go through pharmacy or your institution's override process.** If you think there's an exceptional reason for this dose, a clinical pharmacist or the medical director would need to review it. I can't bypass that safeguard.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
